34.12.m:
He is initiated for twelve [days].
Then indeed he is born;
for that is truly born which is born from austerity,
not that which is from a woman. He is initiated for twelve [days].
Twelve indeed are the impure things in man:
hair, skin, blood, meat, bone, marrow, serum, spleen, tears, rheum, sweat, and what he urinates.
These twelve indeed are the impure things in man.
In that he is initiated for twelve [days],
he strikes them away.
He is initiated for twelve [days];
there are twelve Upasads, and twelve [days] after being pressed; these amount to thirty-six.
The Bṛhatī has thirty-six syllables;
cattle belong to the Bṛhatī;
in the Bṛhatī he thus reaches cattle;
having reached cattle in the Bṛhatī, he secures them.
Then the four syllables that step out, that is the four-footed Anuṣṭubh.
Speech is the Anuṣṭubh;
in speech he thus reaches cattle;
having reached cattle in speech, he secures them.
Therefore cattle recognize their name by speech;
by speech they exist, accomplished.

36.1.m:
When Indra had slain Vṛtra, his virile power and vigor departed.
It entered into all this: the waters, the plants, the trees.
With that the gods exerted themselves.
They brought this together, which is the Sānnāyya.
Curd and milk indeed are a pair.
What was mixed, appearing like scum, like whey, that was the embryo itself.
From heat (tapas) indeed offspring are born;
it attains heat-nature when it attains cooked-nature;
from that indeed one is propagated.
Because he created offspring with the curd-mixture for the Viśve Devas, therefore these offspring belong to the Viśve Devas.
The drops indeed are the Viśve Devas;
them indeed he sacrifices to in this.
For offspring live off what is given from yonder.
This indeed is the womb of offspring.
The Maruts desired it;
they were emitted seized by distress.
Because it is for the Svatavants,
[it is] for independence, for expiation.
The Maruts indeed are cattle;
this is for the propagation of cattle.
It is on seven potsherds,
for the Maruts are seven,
for cutting off.
[It is] for the Maruts;
moreover, he thereby secures village food.
Created by the Vaiśvadeva, they dispersed in different directions;
he encompassed them with [the cake] for Heaven and Earth,
just for the encompassing of the created offspring.
Because it is on one potsherd,
thereby it belongs to Prajāpati;
he thereby firmly establishes the deities toward Prajāpati.
The one-potsherd [cake] indeed is the sacrificer;
the Āhavanīya is the heavenly world;
he offers it as a complete oblation;
he causes him, having become an oblation, to go to the heavenly world.
The one-potsherd [cake] is the self;
ghee is cattle.
When he fills it up with ghee,
he enriches him with cattle.
If he were to fill it completely, cattle would attend on him without giving enjoyment,
for he would make him lower than the cattle.
It should be made with visible back;
he makes him higher than the cattle;
cattle attend on him giving enjoyment.
That is not to be heeded:
he should just fill it up,
for cattle indeed do give enjoyment.
He places it on the barhis,
for firm standing.
When the sacrificer totters, the offspring totter after him;
when the sacrificer stands firm, they stand firm after him.
It should be offered standing straight and firm,
for the firm standing of the sacrificer.

36.4.m:
He sacrifices to the Vājins;
the Vājins indeed are cattle;
them he sacrifices then.
This is not quite like a well-performed sacrifice;
for when the sacrifice has concluded and the enclosing-sticks have been thrown into the fire, he offers.
Because the Hotṛ sacrifices without drawing breath,
thereby the sacrifice is performed.
He offers an after-sacrifice;
Agni is the finished work, Agni is the firm foundation;
[it is] for the firm foundation of the finished work.
The Vājins indeed are cattle;
if he were to conclude, he would conclude them;
for un-concluded, they are continually propagated.
Pouring along the sacrificial grass, he takes [the whey];
he places semen in the bulls.
Offspring, indeed, are the sacrificial grass; semen is the whey (vājina);
he places semen in the offspring.
Sitting with knees raised, he sacrifices;
for the animal has its knees raised;
he places semen in the animal.
Having invited one another, they consume it;
for this is like a Soma-draught.
All the priests eat of it, [thinking:]
“May the Vājins bear my sacrifice.”
He should eat of it himself;
he places vigor in his own self.

36.8.m:
Having created creatures and having sacrificed away distress, he desired: “May I slay Vṛtra.”
Having joined together with these deities—the Maruts as the folk, Agni as the vanguard—he advanced.
Having approached Vṛtra and having seen Vṛtra, he stood paralyzed in the thighs, not daring to attack.
The Maruts charged upon him with wind-chariots of reed;
they went over [him].
Where they reached his vital part, there he writhed;
then indeed they scorched him thoroughly;
therefore [these offerings are] the Sāṃtapanas.
The Sākamedhas are destroying Vṛtra.
With Agni as the vanguard, Indra slew Vṛtra,
precisely for having a vanguard.
This Agni is the commander of the gods' army;
this [offering] is indeed for raising the army.
Indra could not raise the thunderbolt against Vṛtra;
he poured out this portion for the Maruts;
the Maruts scorched him thoroughly for heroic strength;
with that heroic strength he raised the thunderbolt;
then indeed they scorched him thoroughly;
therefore [they are] the Sāṃtapanas.
It is to be poured out at midday,
for then he scorches both ends.
It is a caru,
for he scorches it all around.
The gods did not find Vṛtra’s vital part;
the Maruts penetrated him with their razor-sharp fellies;
then indeed they scorched him thoroughly;
therefore [they are] the Sāṃtapanas.

36.9.m:
About to slay Vṛtra tomorrow, they fasted nearby.
They said: “To whom indeed will this [world] belong tomorrow? For whom will there be cooking?”
They cooked this boiled rice.
They looked out for the cattle: “Whichever of these two [sides] they shall favor tomorrow, they shall be the conquerors.”
With this [rice] they went forth towards them;
with this they held them;
this is the path of cattle.
They dwelt [there], victorious on the morrow.
They said: “To whom indeed will this [world] belong tomorrow? For whom will there be cooking?”
They cooked this boiled rice.
They said: “Let us not eat what has not been offered.”
They offered to the Maruts, the Gṛhamedhas;
the Maruts, the Gṛhamedhas, are cattle;
to them indeed they offered then.
They were arrayed for battle.
The Asuras sent hunger against the gods.
The gods, having heard it coming, cooked this boiled rice.
Not having found a place among the gods, it entered the Asuras again;
it burned them up.
Even in the vicinity, one should cook boiled rice;
he sends hunger against his rival;
it burns him up.
What belongs to the Cāturmāsyas, that of theirs is cattle-bringing, like a domestic sacrifice.
The Gṛhamedhīya is indeed a domestic sacrifice.
Neither fore-sacrifices nor after-sacrifices are offered;
he does not recite the kindling verses.
He offers the two butter portions;
thereby the sacrifice is performed.
He cuts off [a portion] together for Agni.
Agni is the finished work;
Agni is the firm foundation;
[it is] for the firm foundation of the finished work.
The Gṛhamedhas are cattle;
the Iḍā is cattle;
therefore they invoke the Iḍā.
The scraped-out residue is set down,
for continuity.
Indra dwelt away from both of them.
The gods deposited this portion for Indra:
“Having his portion set down, may he choose us tomorrow.”
The roar is the vaṣaṭ-call.
Moreover, they then called away the bull of the Asuras:
“May he propagate us.”
He calls upon the bull;
he thereby invokes Indra;
and he also wrenches away the prosperity of his rival.

36.10.m:
The cows dwell with their calves
for the sake of Sākamedha-nature.
If the wife were not to eat, they would not be Sākamedhas;
in that the wife eats,
[it is] for the sake of Sākamedha-nature.
Nirṛti indeed seizes that of the sacrifice which a woman eats;
a woman indeed is Nirṛti;
this ladle is the hand of a woman.
In that he offers with the ladle,
he appeases Nirṛti by means of Nirṛti herself.
Nirṛti indeed seizes that part of the sacrifice which does not attain proper consecration;
the ladle is indeed seized by Nirṛti,
for it moves about in what is heated.
In that he offers with the ladle,
he appeases Nirṛti by means of [that which is] seized by Nirṛti.
He advanced to slay Vṛtra tomorrow;
the Maruts came sporting around him;
they dispelled his panic;
they emboldened him;
therefore [they are] the Krīḍins.
They were arrayed for battle;
the gods did not see the far end of the Asuras;
they saw the Maruts, the Krīḍins, sporting;
they knew: “These indeed have minds bent on victory.”
For them they poured out this portion;
thence they conquered;
this [offering] is indeed for victory.
Yonder sun indeed is Indra;
the rays are the Krīḍins;
they perform [the rite] together with the rays
for victory.
The gods indeed did not perceive that Vṛtra was slain;
the Maruts sported over him;
therefore [they are] the Krīḍins.
Then these five oblations
[are] for continuity.
Then [the cake] for Indra and Agni;
Indra and Agni indeed hurled the thunderbolt at him;
also they say: “Indra and Agni held the thunderbolt for him.”
Then [the cake] for Indra;
with this Indra indeed slew Vṛtra;
he took up this choice portion;
it is indeed his choice portion;
it is indeed [his] share.
Then [the cake] for Viśvakarman;
[saying,] “All my deeds are done,”
for having slain Vṛtra he became Viśvakarman.
He pours the libation with: “May Agni relish the hotṛ’s office, may he relish the message,”
for the fulfillment of the oblations, to make [them] thirty.

36.11.m:
Having created creatures, having sacrificed away distress, and having slain Vṛtra, they desired immortality;
the heavenly world indeed is immortality.
The year is twelve months, the heavenly world;
in that there are twelve oblations,
he thereby wins immortality.
He indeed has cut short this year;
he indeed has fallen into distress with regard to this year.
In that he brings about six and six [oblations],
six indeed are the seasons;
he thereby restores the seasons;
he follows along as they are restored;
thence he is propagated.
He indeed has surpassed this year;
in that there are twelve oblations,
he turns back towards the year.
He indeed has fallen into distress with regard to this year;
the fathers are the seasons;
he propagates him [the year];
he is propagated in accordance with him as he is being propagated.
This year of his has indeed been obtained, the seasons obtained;
now his fathers are unobtained;
in that this is the sacrifice to the fathers,
his fathers thereby become obtained and pleased.
[The oblation] is to be poured out from the south,
for the south belongs to the fathers.
[Others say] it is to be poured out from both sides,
for both are sacrificed to;
that is not to be heeded;
it is to be poured out only from the south,
for the south belongs to the fathers.
The cake is on six potsherds;
six indeed are the seasons;
the seasons indeed become obtained and pleased by him through this.
Not indeed with parched grains, nor with the mash is it a sacrifice;
in that there is this cake, by that the sacrifice is performed.
Now these parched grains:
these are indeed nectar in yonder world, the boundless nights of the year;
by these, they indeed become obtained and pleased by him.
Not indeed with parched grains, nor with the cake is it a sacrifice to the fathers;
in that there is this mash, by that it is a sacrifice to the fathers.
It is prepared in the milk of a cow that suckles an adopted calf,
for she belongs to the fathers.
He stirs it up closest [to himself] with one [stalk],
for the fathers are in one world;
he stirs it up towards the south,
for the south belongs to the fathers;
he stirs it up without touching [the bottom],
for that reaches the fathers.

36.12.m:
[The ground] is not to be dug up towards the east,
for it is a sacrifice to the fathers;
nor towards the south,
for it is a sacrifice.
He digs up intermediate between both directions,
for both are sacrificed to;
he reaches both, the gods and the fathers.
The altar is four-cornered,
for the fathers are in all quarters.
They sacrifice within an enclosure,
for the fathers are concealed from yonder sun;
also, the fathers are concealed from both gods and men.
He cuts the sacrificial grass close to the root;
thereby [it belongs] to the fathers;
in that [he cuts] without the root,
thereby [it belongs] to the gods;
both are sacrificed to.
He goes around, strewing the sacrificial grass all around,
for these seasons are encircled all around.
He who goes around the sacrifice to the fathers indeed departs from this world;
in that he goes around again without strewing,
he returns to this very world.
They sacrifice to the deities in that former [fire];
if he were to sacrifice there, he would mix the oblations;
he would cause strife.
They make [the oblation] cooked on the Gārhapatya;
thereby the sacrifice is performed.
They bring fire from the rice-cooking [fire];
is it indeed brought from anywhere else?
He recites: “Yearning, we call upon thee,”
for the fathers are yearning.
He recites an anuṣṭubh;
the anuṣṭubh is the end;
the fathers are the end;
also the anuṣṭubh is all the metres.
He recites one [verse],
for the fathers are in one world;
he recites [it] three times,
for the fathers are in the third world.
In that he recites one,
thereby [it belongs] to the fathers;
in that he recites [it] three times,
thereby [it belongs] to the gods;
both are sacrificed to.
He chooses neither the ārṣeya nor the hotṛ;
he releases them from death.
He offers the fore-sacrifices without the sacrificial grass;
the sacrificial grass is indeed offspring;
he releases the offspring from death.
Intending to act for men, one puts on the sacred thread normally;
for the gods, he wears it over the right shoulder;
now here it should be worn over the right shoulder [as prācīnāvīta],
for distinction.
Having sliced off [the portions] from the south, stepping beyond to the north, turning southwards, he offers,
for the south belongs to the fathers.

36.13.m:
He sacrifices to Soma first;
Soma indeed is the deity of the fathers,
for Soma has the fathers as his deity.
In that he sacrifices to Soma accompanied by the fathers,
he thereby sacrifices to the Soma-drinking fathers.
In that [he sacrifices to those] seated on the sacrificial grass, [he sacrifices] to those who sacrificed;
in that [he sacrifices to the] Agniṣvāttas, [he sacrifices] to the householders;
in that [he sacrifices to] Agni, the bearer of the Kavyas—
two indeed are Agni's bodies:
with the one that bears the Havyas he bears the oblation to the gods, with the one that bears the Kavyas to the fathers.
Agni is the Samiṣṭi, Agni is the firm foundation;
[it is] for the Samiṣṭi, for the firm foundation.
He cuts portions five times each;
for five are the seasons.
He utters the vaṣaṭ-call as 'Svadhā, namaḥ';
for svadhā is for the fathers, namaskāra for the gods.
Two indeed are the yājyā and the anuvākyā of the gods;
with the one he holds forth, with the other he causes [it] to reach [them].
Now here three are to be made,
for separation:
two anuvākyās, one yājyā.
Farther indeed than the gods are the fathers;
that which is the third, with it indeed he reaches out.
The human fathers follow in pursuit of the divine fathers;
he has thereby sacrificed to the divine fathers.
In that he places [the portions] on the corners,
he thereby sacrifices to the human fathers.
He places [them] three times;
for we here look towards three men,
[and] he mentions three beyond [them];
three indeed are the father, the son, the grandson:
for continuity.
He places [them] on all the corners;
he causes [them] to reach all the quarters;
for in all the quarters are the fathers.
He should not place [any] on this [corner];
if he were to place [any] on this [corner], he would encompass them with death.
They follow along that very [direction].
In that he wipes [his hand] on it,
thereby indeed he gratifies them.
With, 'Here, fathers, take delight!' they go out;
they approach the Āhavanīya,
with, 'May we [see] you of good appearance.'
They stand until breathless.
Those who approach the sacrifice to the fathers go to the end of their breath;
having made Agni their overseer, they go to the end of their breath.
With, 'Depart, fathers, fond of Soma!' they come back.
The fathers are indeed attached to them;
[this is] for separation.
With, 'The fathers have taken delight,' they enter.
They throw down a tuft of wool or a fringe of cloth,
for the satisfying of whatever they may have met with there.
He sprinkles water around;
he cleanses indeed.
He walks around again without sprinkling around.
Those who approach the sacrifice to the fathers go to yonder world;
only Prajāpati can lead them up from there.
In that he recites this unexpressed [verse] addressed to Prajāpati: 'By this position generating works...',
Prajāpati himself leads them up from there.
The sacrifice has indeed gone to the fathers;
they return with the Paṅkti;
the sacrifice is fivefold (pāṅkta);
they return together with the sacrifice.
His mind indeed goes to the fathers;
with, 'Now we call back our mind,'
he calls it back to himself.

36.14.m:
For him the year has now been won through sacrifice; the fathers have been won through sacrifice;
now the Rudras are not yet won through sacrifice for him;
the Tryambakas are the Rudras.
In that these are the Tryambakas,
thereby for him the Rudras become won through sacrifice [and] pleased.
They are one for each person;
he appeases Rudra for each person.
There is one extra;
he thereby appeases Rudra for the embryos.
They are on one potsherd;
one cannot reach a person with potsherds;
he reaches him in a single way.
They ponder: 'Is butter to be poured over [them], or is butter not to be poured over [them]?'
Butter is indeed to be poured over [them],
for an oblation is not without butter poured over it.
If he were to pour butter over [them], he would bring Rudra down upon the cattle;
therefore butter is not to be poured over [them].
They carry a single firebrand;
for a single firebrand belongs to the Rudras.
They carry it while it smokes;
for what smokes belongs to the Rudras.
They carry it facing away;
they carry Rudra away.
They carry it to this quarter;
in this quarter indeed are the dwellings of the Rudras;
he appeases Rudra in his own dwellings.
With, 'O Rudra, I make the rat thy beast,' he strews one [cake] on a molehill;
he thereby appeases Rudra away from the cattle;
therefore Paśupati is dangerous to them.
They sacrifice at a crossroad;
at a crossroad indeed are the dwellings of the Rudras;
he appeases Rudra in his own dwellings.
With, 'This is thy portion, O Rudra, together with thy sister Ambikā; enjoy it, svāhā!'
Autumn indeed is Rudra's sister Ambikā;
he roams along after her;
therefore he slays most in autumn.
Along with her indeed he appeases him.
He offers with a middle leaf [of the palāśa];
for that is unhurt by the Rakṣases.
If he were to offer with [something] domestic, he would bring Rudra down upon the cattle;
therefore it is offered with [something] wild.
With, 'We have appeased Rudra, O mother,'
they have become free from debt.
With, '[Auspicious] medicine for ox, horse, and man,'
having become free from debt, they make medicine.
With, 'We sacrifice to Tryambaka,' they walk around.
She who may be desirous of a husband should also walk around;
they bring about the finding of a husband for her.
Having tossed them up, they catch them;
having become free from debt, they attain good fortune.
They pour them out together for the sacrificer;
they pour out good fortune together for him.
If [a woman] is desirous of a husband, they should pour them out together for her;
they pour out good fortune together for her.
Having placed them in a net-bag, they hang them on a tree with, 'This is thy portion, O Rudra; with this provision go beyond the Mūjavants!'
The mountain indeed is Rudra's womb;
from there he roams down;
by his own portion he causes him to go to his own womb.
Without looking back they return,
so as not to be pursued by Rudra.
With, 'Fuel art thou; may we prosper,'
having appeased Rudra, they approach prosperity.
With, 'A kindling-stick art thou; may we completely prosper,'
he utters a blessing.
With, 'Splendor art thou; place splendor in me,'
he places splendor in himself.
Ambī indeed is a woman whose name is good fortune (bhagā);
therefore [they are called] Tryambakas.
He indeed is without a firm foundation whose oblation has no firm foundation; the Tryambakas have no firm foundation:
no fore-sacrifices are offered, nor after-sacrifices;
he does not recite the kindling-verses.
He pours out a mess of boiled rice for Aditi;
Aditi indeed is this [earth];
this [earth] is the firm foundation;
on it indeed he finds a firm foundation.

37.2.m:
There is a cake on eight potsherds for Agni, a caru for Soma, a cake on eight potsherds for Savitṛ, a caru for Bṛhaspati, a cake on eleven potsherds for Agni and Soma, a caru for Sarasvatī, a barley cake on ten potsherds for Varuṇa, [and] a cake on one potsherd for Heaven and Earth.
In that it is for Agni:
for the Brahmin belongs to Agni.
In that it is for Soma:
for the Brahmin belongs to Soma.
In that it is for Savitṛ:
that for Savitṛ is solely for instigation.
In that it is for Bṛhaspati:
Bṛhaspati is the brahman;
this is the Brahmin's lordship of speech.
In that it is for Agni and Soma:
for the Brahmin belongs to Agni and to Soma;
when these two unite, then he becomes vigorous.
In that it is for Sarasvatī:
Sarasvatī is speech;
this is the Brahmin's visible lordship of speech.
In that it is for Varuṇa:
that for Varuṇa is solely for freedom from Varuṇa;
for whoever he may be that is consecrated, he belongs to Varuṇa.
In that it is for Heaven and Earth:
Indra raised his thunderbolt against Vṛtra;
Heaven and Earth did not assent to him;
they assented to him with this share;
this is the approval of the thunderbolt;
[it is done] that he may be consecrated with approved thunderbolt.
In that there are eight oblations:
the gāyatrī has eight syllables;
the gāyatrī is the brahman;
[it is done] that he may be consecrated with the brahman.
He is consecrated upon a black antelope skin;
for this is the form of the Ṛc and the Sāman;
in that there is a black antelope skin,
he is consecrated upon the Ṛc and the Sāman.
He purifies the ghee with gold,
solely for brilliance, for luster.
When he is consecrated with ghee,
he thereby attains pre-eminence.

37.3.m:
There is a cake on eight potsherds for Agni, a caru for Pūṣan, a cake on eight potsherds for Savitṛ, a caru for the Viśve Devas, a cake on eight potsherds for Tvaṣṭṛ, a barley cake on ten potsherds for Varuṇa, a cake on seven potsherds for the Maruts, [and] a dappled barren cow for the Maruts.
In that it is for Agni:
that for Agni is for success;
for success has Agni at its head.
In that it is for Pūṣan:
Pūṣan is prosperity;
prosperity belongs to the Vaiśya.
In that it is for Savitṛ:
that for Savitṛ is solely for instigation.
In that it is for the Viśve Devas:
for the Vaiśya belongs to the Viśve Devas.
In that it is for Tvaṣṭṛ:
for Tvaṣṭṛ transforms forms.
In that it is for Varuṇa:
that for Varuṇa is solely for freedom from Varuṇa;
for whoever he may be that is consecrated, he belongs to Varuṇa.
In that it is for the Maruts:
for the Vaiśya belongs to the Maruts.
In that there are seven oblations:
for the Maruts are seven.
Then there is this dappled barren cow for the Maruts;
portions from it are cut together for Agni;
then he is consecrated.
The Maruts are the common people;
he is consecrated from the midst of the people;
therefore he is dear to the people;
for he is consecrated from the midst of the people.
He is consecrated upon a bull's hide;
for he is a procreator,
for procreation.
He is consecrated with curds;
curds are soft;
he makes him soft.

37.4.m:
"We praise the greatness of Narāśaṃsa among these, of the adorable one, through sacrifices, / the gods of good resolve, pure, mindful, who relish both oblations."
He who sacrifices with Soma has a consecration of the gods;
he who sacrifices with an animal has a consecration of the gods;
he who sacrifices with an iṣṭi has a consecration of men.
The gods assented to this for Pṛthī;
with it they consecrated him;
he was consecrated also over wild animals.
As many creatures as there are that speak speech, over all of them is he consecrated who is consecrated with this.
Having made amends, he is consecrated with this Nārāśaṃsī verse;
for Narāśaṃsa is men.
Whatever in the Rājasūya does not have a high altar, all that is performed here.
Then there is this cake on twenty-one potsherds for the Maruts;
portions from it are cut together for Agni;
then he is consecrated.
Thrice seven are the Maruts;
the Maruts are the common people;
he is consecrated from the midst of the people;
therefore he is dear to the people;
for he is consecrated from the midst of the people.

37.5.m:
"Let your milks gather together, let your vigorous powers gather, let your manly strengths gather, O vanquisher of foes! / Swelling for immortality, O Soma, place the highest glories in heaven!"
There is indeed no consecration of Soma with Soma;
for Soma is [already] consecrated;
for they kill Soma;
for what has been killed is not consecrated.
He is consecrated with a Triṣṭubh addressed to Soma,
for that is the consecration of Soma.
He is consecrated upon a barren cow that has calved, belonging to Soma;
for she is an emitter of semen, and Soma is semen.
Whatever else is in the Rājasūya other than the stomas, all that is performed here.
Then there is this cake on twenty-one potsherds for the Maruts;
from it a portion is cut off for Agni.
Then he is consecrated.
Thrice seven are the Maruts;
the Maruts are the people;
he is consecrated from the midst of the people themselves.
Therefore is he dear to the people;
for he is consecrated from the midst of the people.

37.6.m:
What is the Rājasūya, that is the consecration of Varuṇa;
what is the Vājapeya, that is the consecration of the universal ruler.
Prajāpati is universal rule, Parameṣṭhin;
sovereignty is the cow itself;
that is this Gosava.
Just like a cow does he become who is consecrated with this.
He is consecrated with freshly milked milk;
for that is sovereignty.
The Bṛhat is the sāman;
for that is sovereignty.
Ten thousand is the sacrificial fee;
for that is sovereignty.
In the Pavamāna [lauds] they introduce the Kaṇvarathaṃtara.
To the south of the Āhavanīya, on [ground] that has not been dug up, he is consecrated.
This [earth] is the cow;
unseparated from her is he consecrated.
The stoma is thirty-six-fold;
this is indeed an animal-stoma;
therefore it is the Gosava.

37.7.m:
The stoma is ninefold;
the ninefold is spiritual luster;
with spiritual luster he is consecrated.
The Rathaṃtara is the sāman;
the Rathaṃtara is spiritual luster;
with spiritual luster he is consecrated.
The Agniṣṭoma is the stoma;
the Agniṣṭoma is spiritual luster;
with spiritual luster he is consecrated.
The Pravargya is performed;
the Pravargya is spiritual luster;
with spiritual luster he is consecrated.
The Hotṛ wears a garland;
he is reddish, blinking, [having studied] the Triśukriya;
this is the visible form of spiritual luster;
by its visible form he secures spiritual luster.
This is indeed a single pressing, in that there is one stoma and one meter.
He should give eleven at each pressing,
for the differentiation of the pressings.
They amount to thirty-three;
thirty-three are the deities;
he secures the deities.
He gives a horse as the thirty-fourth;
Prajāpati is the thirty-fourth;
he reaches Prajāpati.
The gods did not have faith in Bṛhaspati;
Prajāpati caused him to sacrifice with this;
then they had faith in him.
That is this Bṛhaspatisava.
One who desires spiritual luster should sacrifice with this;
he becomes possessed of spiritual luster.
For him this is the rule:
all his offspring become Brahmins possessed of spiritual luster.

37.8.m:
The stoma is fifteenfold;
the fifteenfold is vigor and virility;
with vigor and virility he is consecrated.
The Bṛhat is the sāman;
the Bṛhat is vigor and virility;
with vigor and virility he is consecrated.
The Ukthya is the stoma;
the Ukthas are vigor and virility;
with vigor and virility he is consecrated.
The stotra and the śastra belong to Indra, and the Pavamānas are in the Triṣṭubh meter;
the Triṣṭubh is vigor and virility;
with vigor and virility he is consecrated.
All adversaries, all destructions, all demons wished to slay Indra;
Prajāpati caused him to sacrifice with this;
he overcame all adversaries, all destructions, all demons.
Indra is the sacrificer;
in that he sacrifices with this,
he overcomes all adversaries, all destructions, all demons.
That is this Indrasava.

37.10.m:
May Bodha and Pratībodha protect me from the east;
and may Asvapna and Anavadrāṇa protect me from the south;
and may Gopāyamāna and Rakṣamāṇa protect me from the west;
and may Jāgṛvi and Arundhatī protect me from the north.
Make us free from enemies below, free from enemies above;
O Indra, make us free from enemies behind, free from enemies in front.
May we accompany days free from enemies; O all-gods, free from enemies may dawns and sunsets be for us.
The six broad ones, the five intermediate directions, may they protect us;
O Mitradhā, establish us in a friend;
let there be fearlessness for us.
You who attend the night and who watch over beings,
who guard all cattle, watch over us in ourselves.
O Bodha, Pratībodha, Asvapna, Anavadrāṇa, Gopāyamāna, Rakṣamāṇa, Jāgṛvi, Arundhatī, you gods who are body-protectors, protect my body here.
'O Bodha, Pratībodha':
that sun, indeed, is Bodha;
Agni is Pratībodha.
'O Asvapna, Anavadrāṇa':
the moon, indeed, is Asvapna;
he who purifies himself is Anavadrāṇa.
'O Gopāyamāna, Rakṣamāṇa':
the day, indeed, is Gopāyamāna;
the night is Rakṣamāṇa.
'O Jāgṛvi, Arundhatī':
the sacrifice, indeed, is Jāgṛvi;
the sacrificial fee is Arundhatī.
These gods, indeed, are the realm-sustainers;
them indeed he praises,
for the protection of himself.

37.15.m:
To the earth, svāhā!
To the intermediate space, svāhā!
To the sky, svāhā!
To desire, svāhā!
To sleep, svāhā!
To the day, svāhā!
To the night, svāhā!
Satisfied are you;
satisfied am I.
Satisfied is my out-breath; satisfied is my diffused breath; satisfied is my in-breath;
satisfied is my sight; satisfied is my hearing; satisfied is my speech; satisfied is my self.
Sky is my father; Earth is my mother;
Sun is my sight; Wind is my breath.
Unconquered by name am I; as such, I entrust this self of mine to Sky and Earth; upon them two I lean, upon them two in heroic stride.
Carry me across unto protection!
Do not harm me!
Protect [me] from the hater; protect from injury;
protect from ungodly defamation!
You are a stone, body-protecting;
as such, protect my body here.
In you is my vulnerable spot, in Bṛhaspati my breath; as such, protect me from death.
Upward speech, upward insight, upward faculty, upward offspring, upward cattle—may he reach this who threatens me from this direction.
May Indra and Agni protect me from this direction;
may the fathers protect me from this direction;
may Savitṛ protect me from this direction;
may Mitra and Varuṇa protect me from this direction;
may Bṛhaspati protect me from this direction.
Life-making, mistress of life, O one of independent power!
Our herdsmen are you, protectors;
may no one dig us out; may we not perish!
